  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWeak mid and upper level ridging is in place today into \ntomorrow though there is a subtle low level shortwave moving in\nfrom the North. This will mainly lead to a few scattered lake \neffect rain showers mainly for the Tug Hill region late this \nafternoon through tonight. Warm air advection through the \novernight hours tonight and tomorrow night will help keep lows a\nlittle warmer than climatology, with mins in the upper 30s and \n40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA trough digging into the central US today along with a weak \nmid level shortwave near the gulf coast will attempt to phase \nlater this week. A surface low begins to slowly deepen in the \nOhio river valley Wednesday night into Thursday, with the warm \nfront lifting into the Northeast Wednesday night. This will \nbring widespread rain to most of the region. With recent trends \nin models indicating a poor phase between the midwest trough and\ngulf coast shortwave the low will likely not deepen quickly. \nThis means that even though the low is likely going to pass \nsouth of our region, the cold air aloft does not filter in quick\nenough to transition the precipitation to snow until the low is\nwell east of us and most of the precipitation has come to an \nend. 850 mb temperatures below freezing do not start filtering \nin until late Thursday into Thursday night, with the overall \ncold air advection weak so deep enough cold air for snow to \nvalley floors may not occur till Friday Morning. By then there \nwill only be some residual showers though wrap around \nprecipitation may keep some snow going in the Catskills. A \ndeeper core of colder air moves in Saturday, with favorable \nconditions for Lake Effect Precipitation. Near the lake shore, \nthe water temperatures may keep the precipitation mostly rain, \nincluding the NY thruway Corridor. The higher elevations south \nof I-90 in CNY will see snow but warm ground temperatures, \nsnowfall during the day, and snow fall rates below a half inch \nper hour in the heavier showers will prevent any accumulation \nfrom happening.\n\nHigh pressure builds in late this weekend into early next week \nwith a gradual moderation of temperatures while remaining mostly\ndry.",
